// In-depth battles, batch ac: Tacna, Arica, Majuba, Geok Tepe, Tel el-Kebir, Cartum.

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "mahdi": "Title of a religious leader who, in Islamic tradition, will come to guide the faithful. In Sudan, Muhammad Ahmad declared himself the Mahdi in the early 1880s and raised the country against the Egyptian government.",
  "heliógrafo": "A mirror that reflects sunlight to send coded signals over many miles. It was the telegraph of armies in the field.",
  "terreno morto": "The part of a slope that cannot be seen from above, because the ground curves away. Anyone climbing there is hidden from the fire of those on top.",
  "suserania": "The power of a strong state over another, which has its own government but cannot decide certain things alone, such as foreign policy.",
  "teke": "One of the great Turkmen peoples of Central Asia, horse breeders and warriors, who lived in the Akhal oasis, in present-day Turkmenistan.",
  "quediva": "Title of the ruler of Egypt from 1867 to 1914. He was a vassal of the Ottoman sultan but in practice ruled on his own, and after 1882 came to depend on the British.",
  "retrocarga": "A weapon loaded from the rear of the barrel instead of the muzzle. It reloads faster, and you can do it lying down, under cover.",
  "bôeres": "The descendants of Dutch settlers in southern Africa. They were farmers and hunters, and excellent shots. The word comes from the Dutch 'boer', meaning farmer."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  tacna: {
    war: "War of the Pacific (1879–1884)",
    hook: "The biggest battle of the War of the Pacific: a sandy plateau, five hours of fire and an alliance that fell apart in a single afternoon.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1879, Chile, Peru and Bolivia went to war over the Atacama desert, rich in [[saltpeter|salitre]]. This is the [[War of the Pacific|guerra do pacífico]]. Chile occupied the Bolivian coast right at the start and took the initiative. By the end of 1879 it had conquered the Peruvian province of Tarapacá, and the Chilean government expected a defeated Peru to ask for terms. It did not.",
        "The two allied countries were going through crises. The loss of Tarapacá cut off Peru's saltpeter income, and the defeat at Camarones shook morale. In Peru, Nicolás de Piérola took the place of President Mariano Ignacio Prado. In Bolivia, General Narciso Campero replaced Hilarión Daza. On the Chilean side, General Erasmo Escala left the command after quarreling with the Minister of War, Rafael Sotomayor, and Manuel Baquedano took over. Sotomayor died on 20 May 1880, six days before the battle, and José Francisco Vergara took his place.",
        { h: "The road to Tacna" },
        { timeline: [
          ["February 1880", "About 10,000 Chilean soldiers land at Ilo, on the coast of Peru."],
          ["Late March", "The Chileans take the hill of Los Ángeles and advance inland."],
          ["19 April", "Campero takes command of the allies."],
          ["25 May, night", "The allies try an ambush at Quebrada Honda, but fog and darkness spoil the plan."],
          ["26 May", "Battle at the Alto de la Alianza, near Tacna."]
        ]},
        "The allies gathered between 9,000 and 12,000 men between Tacna and Arica. The command was tangled: Campero, a Bolivian, was commander in general, but the Peruvian rear admiral Lizardo Montero ended up confirmed as commander in chief. In April, Montero wanted to wait for the attack at Tacna, and the Bolivian colonel Eliodoro Camacho wanted to ambush the Chileans in the Sama valley. The defensive position won out."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Manuel Baquedano, Chilean general" },
        "He took command after the quarrel between Escala and Minister Sotomayor. Before the battle, he chose Velásquez's plan: a simultaneous frontal attack, instead of the flanking maneuver Vergara proposed. It was a simple plan, and it depended on the men arriving in one piece after a march across the desert.",
        { h: "Narciso Campero and Lizardo Montero, the allies" },
        "Campero, a Bolivian general, held the overall command. Montero, a Peruvian admiral, commanded the right of the line. The alliance joined two armies with different weapons and ammunition. The rifles were of many models, many of them old, and the ammunition of one army did not always fit the other.",
        { h: "Eliodoro Camacho and Miguel Castro Pinto" },
        "Camacho, a Bolivian colonel, commanded the allied left, and Castro Pinto, also Bolivian, the center. Camacho had argued for the ambush at the Sama. The heavy fighting began on his wing, and the order for the general counterattack is credited to him."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers vary widely. On the Chilean side, 14,147 men were available, but the historians Encina and Castedo count only 9,645 in combat, with about 37 guns. On the allied side, the Peruvian Jorge Basadre gives 8,930 men (4,705 Peruvians and 4,225 Bolivians), and Chilean historians cite about 12,500. Campero believed the Chileans had 22,000.",
        { note: "The allied artillery also changes in number depending on the source: 31 pieces in one, 16 in another, nine guns and eight machine guns in a third. That is why we do not pick a number." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The battle was fought at the Alto de la Alianza (also called Intiorko), a sandy plateau next to Tacna. The slopes were gentle, and the sand ridges hid the reserves. The allied line was 2.5 to 3 kilometers long, with four [[redoubts|reduto]], and almost no trenches, only small sand works for the guns. The west wing was almost impassable for artillery and infantry, and the road from Sama to Tacna protected the other side.",
        { h: "The night before" },
        "On the night of 25 May, told by prisoners that the Chilean army was bigger than they had thought, the allies marched to attack by night at Quebrada Honda. The fog wrecked the march: they went back to their positions at around 5 in the morning, with units lost along the way. A Chilean battalion, the Atacama, even fired on them as they withdrew. The allies began the day tired.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the Chilean advance. Baquedano put three divisions in front, side by side: Amengual (first) on the left, Barceló (second) in the center and Barbosa (fourth) on the right. The third division, under Amunátegui, came behind, and Muñoz Bezanilla's reserve was further back still. The advance began at around 8 in the morning, and the guns opened fire close to 9. The artillery duel was weak, because many shots buried themselves in the sand.",
        "Second, the clash on the allied left. Between 10 and 11 (sources differ), Amengual's division hit Camacho's position. The Bolivian battalions fell back, and Camacho threw into the line the reserves he had. The Bolivian cavalry dismounted to fight on foot. The Sucre battalion lost, according to the accounts, about 80% of its men.",
        "Third, the Chilean crisis. Barceló's division got to within about 80 meters of the allied trenches with little ammunition, and fell back in disorder. Camacho ordered the general counterattack, with the Peruvian divisions of Cáceres and Suárez and Castro Pinto's infantry. It was the moment when the battle was most evenly balanced.",
        "Fourth, the Bolivian battalions Colorados and Aroma. They formed a [[square|quadrado]] and repelled the Chilean cavalry, including a Granaderos charge that failed, although it bought time for the rest of the army. The Colorados even captured Chilean guns. The Chilean artillery lost many men and several pieces.",
        "Fifth, the third division enters. Amunátegui's division, which had been coming up behind, reinforced the Chilean left together with the Marine Artillery. The crossfire of these troops, added to what was left of Amengual's men, destroyed the Bolivian units. Muñoz's reserve, far behind, hardly fought: it had only 17 wounded.",
        "Sixth, the collapse. Barbosa's division attacked the allied right in open order, with skirmishers spread out. The Cazadores del Desierto regiment went around Montero's position, and the Chileans took the artillery pieces. Montero threw his last reserve, Del Solar's division, into the fight, and fell back. Camacho was wounded and replaced by Ramón González, which shook the left even more. At around 2:30 p.m., the last redoubts fell. The Colorados held out until about 3:30 p.m. In all, the fighting lasted some five hours. The Chilean cavalry pursued the fugitives and took about 180 prisoners. Amengual's division entered Tacna at dusk, after the city had been bombarded."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Chilean losses come to around 2,000 men: Francisco Machuca adds up 474 dead and 1,458 wounded, and the Chilean army records give 2,028 casualties. Allied losses are far less certain. Basadre estimates about 185 officers killed and some 2,000 soldiers lost, and Chilean estimates reach 3,500 to 5,000. Of the Colorados, 293 men were left, and the Aroma battalion lost 388.",
        "Campero withdrew to Bolivia, reaching La Paz on 10 June. Montero withdrew to Puno.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Tacna took Bolivia out of the war. Peru was left alone, and Chile understood that only a Peruvian surrender would end the conflict. A few days later, the last allied position in the region, Arica, was attacked. The Treaty of Ancón, of 20 October 1883, ended the war and gave Tarapacá to Chile. Tacna stayed under Chilean occupation until the Treaty of Lima of 1929, which returned the city to Peru.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The looting: after the victory, Chilean troops looted Tacna, and the foreign consuls protested to Baquedano on 30 May." },
        { note: "The sick: many allied soldiers had gone from the hospital straight to the line." },
        { note: "The numbers: each country tells the battle with its own figures. They agree on the outcome, an allied army undone in about five hours, but not on the size of the armies or of the losses." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  arica: {
    war: "War of the Pacific (1879–1884)",
    hook: "A hill by the sea, an answer that became a motto of the army and less than an hour of assault.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Tacna, Bolivia left the war, and Peru was left alone. Chile needed a port near its army to receive supplies, and Arica, the last Peruvian stronghold in the Tacna region, was the right port. The city lay next to a tall, steep hill, the Morro de Arica, which overlooked the sea.",
        "Colonel Francisco Bolognesi commanded the [[garrison|guarnição]]. Part of the forces that were meant to reinforce him never arrived, and he was cut off by land and by sea.",
        { timeline: [
          ["26 May 1880", "Chile defeats the allies at Tacna."],
          ["2 to 4 June", "Chilean troops arrive by railway. The Peruvian engineer Teodoro Elmore is captured, along with the plans of the mines."],
          ["5 June", "The Chilean envoy asks for surrender. Bolognesi refuses."],
          ["6 June", "Chilean bombardment, by land and sea. In the afternoon, another surrender offer and another refusal."],
          ["7 June", "The assault at dawn."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Francisco Bolognesi, Peruvian colonel" },
        "When the Chilean envoy José de la Cruz Salvo came to ask him to surrender, Bolognesi replied, according to the best-known account, that he had sacred duties to fulfill and would fulfill them until he burned his last cartridge. The phrase became the motto of the Peruvian Army. He died at the top of the Morro.",
        { h: "Pedro Lagos, Chilean colonel" },
        "He commanded the assault, planned with General Manuel Baquedano. He offered surrender twice, on the two days before the attack, and split the infantry into three groups."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Peruvian garrison had about 1,600 to 1,900 men and 19 guns, depending on the source (1,628 in one, 1,858 in another). Of the Chileans, the numbers range from about 4,000 to 6,500, and 5,479 appears in one count.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Two sets of batteries defended the city on the east side (the Ciudadela and the East Fort) and, above all, the Morro de Arica. The Peruvians had [[mined|mina]] the city, with mines meant to be set off from the hospital. The plan of the mines, however, was with Elmore, and his capture told the Chileans where they were.",
        { h: "The morning of 7 June" },
        "The attack began at around 5:30, against the batteries to the east. The Ciudadela and the East Fort fell after a hard defense. The Peruvians tried to regroup on the Morro Gordo, but the Chilean 4th Line Regiment went straight for the summit. According to one account, it was the shout of a soldier, whose identity is unknown, that set off the mass charge up the hill.",
        "Bolognesi died there, along with Captain Juan Guillermo More (sometimes spelled Moore). On the Chilean side, Lieutenant Colonel Juan José San Martín died. The Morro fell at around 7:30, and the final phase of the assault lasted 55 minutes.",
        { h: "What is told of Ugarte" },
        "Colonel Alfonso Ugarte, commander of one of the Peruvian divisions, died on the Morro. According to tradition, he threw himself off the cliff on horseback, so as not to hand over the flag. The accounts vary in the details.",
        { h: "The sea" },
        "The Peruvian monitor Manco Cápac was sunk by its own commander, near the island of Alacrán, so that it would not fall into Chilean hands. A torpedo launch, the Alianza, escaped to the north, but ran aground later, and the crew was captured."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The historian William Sater's estimates for the Peruvian side are 700 to 900 dead, 200 wounded and 1,300 prisoners. The Chileans had about 120 dead and 354 wounded (474 casualties). Other counts speak of about 1,000 Peruvian casualties.",
        "Arica stayed with Chile, which gained the port. Peru lost its southern naval and land base. The fall of the city led to Lynch's Chilean expedition and to the Arica Conference, which failed to end the war.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In Peru, 7 June is Flag Day, and Bolognesi is a national hero. In Chile, the Army remembers the date as Infantry Day. The dispute over Tacna and Arica only ended in 1929, with the Treaty of Lima: Tacna went back to Peru, and Arica stayed with Chile.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The prisoners: in the first moments of the occupation, Chilean soldiers killed Peruvian prisoners near the church. Order only returned when the Lautaro and Bulnes regiments entered the city. Peruvian accounts speak of looting and killings afterward." },
        { note: "The numbers: the garrison and the Chilean forces appear with very different values depending on the author, and the sources do not reconcile them." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  majuba: {
    war: "First Boer War (1880–1881)",
    hook: "Four hundred British soldiers climbed a hill by night and, in a single day, were beaten by farmers.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Transvaal was a republic of farmers of Dutch origin, the [[Boers|bôeres]]. In 1877, Britain annexed the territory, which was in financial crisis and under pressure from the Zulus and the Pedis. Gladstone's Liberal government, which came to power in 1880, kept the annexation, and Boer resentment only grew.",
        "The war began in December 1880, after a tax dispute over a wagon. On 20 December, the Boers ambushed a British column at Bronkhorstspruit: 56 British dead and 92 wounded.",
        { timeline: [
          ["16 December 1880", "The war begins."],
          ["28 January 1881", "General Colley attacks the Boer position at Laing's Nek and is repulsed, with about 150 casualties out of 480 men."],
          ["8 February", "Combat of Schuinshoogte (Ingogo). The British escape narrowly and lose 139 men."],
          ["14 February", "Fighting is suspended for peace talks, proposed by Paul Kruger."],
          ["26 to 27 February", "Colley climbs Majuba hill."]
        ]},
        "Colley's force, the Natal Field Force, had 1,200 to 1,400 men, plus a small naval brigade, artillery and Gatling machine guns. It had very little cavalry, which counted for a lot on that terrain. After two setbacks, the general was looking for a position that would change the game."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "George Colley, British general" },
        "We do not know for certain why Colley climbed Majuba. Historians raise three hypotheses: he may have seen the Boer trenches advancing toward the hill and wanted to occupy it before it was fortified; the Boers suspected he wanted to outflank the position at Laing's Nek; and some think the hill was seen as unclimbable, so that the mere presence of soldiers on top would be enough to frighten the enemy.",
        { h: "Nicolaas Smit and Piet Joubert, Boer commanders" },
        "Joubert was the Boer commander in chief. Smit led the assault groups on Majuba, and historians praise him for using fire-and-movement tactics, in which some shoot while others advance. The group leaders were Stephanus Roos, D. J. K. Malan and Joachim Ferreira.",
        { h: "Ian Hamilton, lieutenant" },
        "An officer of the Gordon Highlanders, he saw the danger growing on the slope and warned his superiors several times. According to the account, he was told to keep quiet."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "About 405 men stayed on top: 171 of the 58th regiment, 141 of the Gordon Highlanders (92nd) and a small naval brigade from the ship Dido. Each man carried 70 cartridges, a full canteen, three days' rations, a greatcoat and a waterproof sheet. Two companies of the 60th, riflemen, stayed on the slopes, as pickets and to guard the line of communication.",
        "The Boers had between 400 and 450 men in the assault groups. They were excellent shots, with [[breech-loading|retrocarga]] rifles, mostly single-shot, and they had no [[bayonet|baioneta]]. They advanced like light horsemen and avoided hand-to-hand fighting.",
        { note: "Colley did not take guns to the top and did not have trenches dug, despite his officers' advice. Each company had only four picks and six shovels, and he expected the Boers to back off. He took a [[heliograph|heliógrafo]], with which he talked to the camp." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Majuba has a curved, convex slope. Anyone on the summit could not see the ground right below: a kneeling soldier often saw only about 45 meters ahead, and in some stretches as much as about 365 meters of slope were hidden. This [[dead ground|terreno morto]] hid those who climbed.",
        { h: "The night and the dawn" },
        "Colley occupied the summit during the night of 26 to 27 February. When day broke, at around 4:30, the Gordon Highlanders spread out across the summit, and some occupied the height on the right, Gordon's Knoll. At first the Boers did not even know the British were there. They noticed when the Gordons shouted and shook their fists, and thought artillery was coming.",
        { h: "The battle in four movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, fire from a distance. Older marksmen, at the foot of the hill and on the low ridges, fired from 800 to 1,200 yards (about 730 to 1,100 meters) at the silhouettes of the soldiers, outlined against the sky, and kept them pinned in place.",
        "Second, the climb through dead ground. Meanwhile, younger volunteers, in groups of 180 to 200 men, climbed where the ground hid them, led by Roos, Malan and Ferreira. They advanced from rock to rock, some shooting and others running.",
        "Third, the still morning. Hamilton warned the command four times (three directly to Colley, once to Major Hay). Colley, unalarmed, did not order a counterattack or reinforce the line.",
        "Fourth, the fall. At 12:45, Ferreira's men opened heavy fire on Gordon's Knoll and took it. Then the Boers reached the edge of the summit and fired down on the British reserves, which were sheltered in a hollow in the middle and were now exposed. Those fleeing the summit flooded into that hollow, and the retreat turned into a rout. Joubert noted afterward that the British rifles were set for 400 to 600 yards, but the fight was at 50 or 100, so the British were shooting over the attackers' heads.",
        "Colley tried a fighting retreat and was killed by Boer marksmen. The soldiers fled down the rear slope, where fire from the summit caught more of them. The Gordons were the ones who held out longest, and the battle ended when they gave way."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The British lost 92 dead, 134 wounded and 59 prisoners (285 casualties out of about 405 men). Among the dead was Captain Cornwallis Maude, son of a government minister. The Boers had 1 dead and 5 wounded (another source gives six wounded, one of them fatally).",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the last battle of the war. A truce came on 6 March, and peace was signed on 23 March 1881. The Pretoria Convention, of 3 August 1881, gave the Transvaal self-government under British [[suzerainty|suserania]]: Britain kept foreign policy. The London Convention of 1884 later widened its independence.",
        "The gold found on the Witwatersrand in 1886 changed the game. The tension led to the Jameson Raid in 1896 and to the Second Boer War in 1899. 'Remember Majuba' became a British war cry.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The negotiation: Colley attacked when peace talks had already begun. One of the texts we consulted calls the decision reckless." },
        { note: "The weight of the defeat: Majuba is remembered as a symbol of British decline, but some point out that the long-term effect on the Empire was limited." },
        { note: "The numbers: the British on the hill are counted as 400 or 405. The Boers, as 400 to 450 in the assault groups, not counting the long-range marksmen." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  geoktepe: {
    war: "Russian conquest of Central Asia (1880–1881)",
    hook: "A mud wall, a mine under the southeast corner and one of the bloodiest conquests of the Russian advance.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 19th century, the Russian Empire was advancing south, through Central Asia. What remained was the Akhal oasis, home of the [[Teke|teke]] people, mounted warriors. In 1879, a Russian expedition was defeated by them at Geok Tepe. The humiliation called for an answer.",
        "The problem was distance. The oasis was surrounded by hundreds of kilometers of semi-desert, and carrying food, water and gunpowder there required thousands of camels. In March 1880, General Mikhail Skobelev took command of the Transcaspian region and chose a slow, heavy strategy instead of a dash.",
        { timeline: [
          ["1879", "A Russian expedition is defeated by the Teke at Geok Tepe."],
          ["March 1880", "Skobelev takes command and starts gathering supplies."],
          ["11 June 1880", "The Russians occupy Bami, the starting base."],
          ["Late December 1880", "Skobelev advances to a fort a few kilometers from Geok Tepe."],
          ["January 1881", "Siege, trenches and mine."],
          ["24 January 1881 (12 January, in the Russian calendar)", "The final assault."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Mikhail Skobelev, Russian general" },
        "His soldiers called him the White General, and the Turks called him the White Pasha. After Geok Tepe, he was removed from command, probably in part because of the slaughter of civilians, and sent to Minsk. He died on 7 July 1882, in Moscow. The official version is a heart attack.",
        { h: "The Teke" },
        "The sources we consulted do not give the name of a Teke commander: the defense was led by tribal chiefs, which is why this text does not invent a name.",
        { h: "Aleksey Kuropatkin" },
        "He arrived on 27 December, with five companies, after a march through the desert from Khiva. In the assault, he commanded the column that entered through the main breach."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "At the end of December 1880, Skobelev had some 4,000 infantry soldiers, 750 cavalry, artillery, rockets, machine guns and heliographs. Estimates of the Russian total range from about 5,000 to 7,200 men, with 72 pieces of artillery. Inside the fortress, between 20,000 and 25,000 people are estimated, men, women and children, of whom some 8,000 had firearms. The Teke had no artillery.",
        { note: "The sources do not even agree on the name of the place. Denghil-Tepe seems to be the fort and a small hill in the northwest corner, and Geok Tepe the wider region." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An enormous mud fortress, more than 4 kilometers around, where the warriors and families stayed. The sources differ on the measurements: one gives walls 5.5 meters thick and 3 high on the inside, with a dry ditch in front; another speaks of walls 6 to 7 meters high and 8 thick at the base; a third, of five sazhens of thickness, which makes about 10 meters.",
        { h: "The siege" },
        "Skobelev advanced slowly. He occupied a fort to the south, Yanghi-Kala, on 1 January, to control the water, and chose the southeast corner as the point of attack. In the following days, the Russians dug a [[parallel|paralela]] about 600 meters from the wall and then another, closer. The Teke made sorties, some successful, but paid for them with many lives. General Petrushevich died as he threw himself at the gate.",
        "The Russian artillery opened a breach in the wall, but the defenders repaired it. Skobelev turned to the [[sappers|sapadores]]: a [[mine|mina]] was dug under the southeast corner and loaded with gunpowder. The amount varies by source: 1,200 kg, 1,180 kg, or two tons.",
        { h: "The assault in five movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the opening. At 7:00, the Russian artillery began to bombard, and a diversionary attack was launched against a redoubt to the west.",
        "Second, the explosion. At 11:20, the mine exploded and opened a breach of about 43 meters in the wall.",
        "Third, the columns. Kuropatkin entered through the breach with about 11 and a half companies and took the position. Kozelkov's column, with eight companies, attacked a smaller breach, to the south, and stalled. It only advanced with reserves and assault ladders on the flanks.",
        "Fourth, the invasion. The two groups joined up and fortified themselves, and the western group scaled the wall. Skobelev then ordered the general advance. By afternoon, the northwest hill had fallen, and the Teke fled over the north wall.",
        "Fifth, the pursuit. The Russian cavalry chased the fugitives for 16 kilometers, until nightfall."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Russian losses vary: Skobelev spoke of 59 dead and 304 wounded on the final day. Another count adds up 268 dead and 669 wounded, and about 645 men died of disease. Of the Teke, estimates range from 15,000 to 20,000 dead, defenders and civilians together. Skobelev's report speaks of 6,500 bodies buried inside the fortress and 8,000 dead in the pursuit, men and women. The Russians spared about 5,000 women and children and freed some 600 Persian slaves.",
        "On 30 January, the Russians took Ashgabat, then a small town. In May 1881, Transcaspia became a province of the Russian Empire. In September, Persia signed the Treaty of Akhal, and the Atrek river became the border. Merv was taken in 1884, and the Panjdeh confrontation came in 1885.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the decisive blow against Turkmen resistance. In present-day Turkmenistan, the battle is a day of mourning and of pride, and in the 1990s a mosque was built to remember the defenders.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The dates: Russia then used the Julian calendar, twelve days behind ours. The assault, 12 January in the Russian calendar, was on 24 January by the calendar we use. The accounts mix the two calendars in the dates of the siege: a Russian encyclopedia gives 23 December 1880 (4 January 1881) for the start of the direct siege of the fortress, and so the dates in December and early January carry a margin of doubt." },
        { note: "The slaughter: Skobelev's own report speaks of thousands of bodies, and he was removed afterward, in part, according to historians, because of the death of so many civilians." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  telelkebir: {
    war: "Anglo-Egyptian War (1882)",
    hook: "A night march in the desert, guided by the stars, and an hour of battle that gave the British Egypt for more than seventy years.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Colonel Ahmed Urabi, an Egyptian officer of peasant origin, led a revolt in 1882 with the slogan 'Egypt for the Egyptians'. London feared for the Suez Canal, the road to India, and decided to intervene.",
        { timeline: [
          ["11 to 13 July 1882", "The British fleet bombards Alexandria, and the marines occupy the city."],
          ["5 August", "British reconnaissance at Kafr el-Dawwar."],
          ["20 August", "Wolseley occupies Ismailia, on the Canal, without a fight."],
          ["Late August", "Combat of Kassassin: the British hold, wait for the heavy cavalry and push the Egyptians back about 8 km."],
          ["13 September", "Battle of Tel el-Kebir."]
        ]},
        "General Garnet Wolseley set up a base at Ismailia and advanced along the freshwater canal and the railway. Urabi dug in at Tel el-Kebir, on the road to Cairo."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Garnet Wolseley, British general" },
        "A veteran of colonial campaigns, he judged that a flanking maneuver across a waterless desert was impractical, and preferred a night approach, to attack head-on at dawn and gain surprise.",
        { h: "Ahmed Urabi, Egyptian colonel" },
        "He led the nationalist movement. He had about 60 guns and [[breech-loading|retrocarga]] rifles, and had a line of trenches and redoubts about five kilometers long dug in the desert. After the battle, he was tried and exiled.",
        { h: "Lieutenant Rawson, of the Navy" },
        "Wolseley chose a Navy officer, Lieutenant Wyatt Rawson, who knew the stars, to guide the Highland Brigade through the night. Rawson was gravely wounded in the assault."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British had about 11,000 infantry soldiers, 2,000 cavalry and 45 guns, including an Indian contingent. The Egyptians had between 13,000 and 20,000 men and some 60 guns, depending on the source.",
        "The British formation was in two divisions side by side: the second, under Hamley, on the left, with the Highland Brigade in front, and the first, under Willis, on the right, with Graham's brigade in front. Behind each, a reserve brigade (in one of them, the Guards Brigade), and the guns between the two. Drury Lowe's cavalry was on the right. An Indian brigade advanced on the other side of the canal.",
        { note: "The Egyptian numbers vary: 13,000 to 18,000 in one count, about 20,000 in another." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An open desert plain, followed by the railway and the freshwater canal. The Egyptian line began with redoubts next to the canal and the railway and ran about five kilometers north, to the end of a stretch of higher ground. A second line of trenches protected the camp, behind.",
        { h: "The night march" },
        "Urabi's army was about ten miles (16 km) from Kassassin. Wolseley marched at night, and the column steered by the stars, because there were no landmarks. There was no moon, but the stars were clear. The march went well, with a drift to the right. At some moments the stars were hidden, and the Highland Brigade briefly lost its way.",
        { note: "On Egyptian vigilance, the sources contradict each other. In one account, Wolseley's reconnaissance showed that the Egyptians did not place outposts at night, which allowed the approach. In another, the Egyptian soldiers were tired from a night on alert and the sentries saw the British at around 5:45. What both have in common is that the surprise was only partial." },
        { h: "The battle in five movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the dawn. When the Highland Brigade came within about 150 yards (about 140 m) of the Egyptian line, fire opened. The Egyptians fired from about 600 yards (some 550 m), but the smoke from the shots spoiled their aim, and the British reached the trenches with few casualties.",
        "Second, the assault. The Highlanders ran to the trenches and went in with the [[bayonet|baioneta]]. The Gordons and the Camerons went in through the center. The Black Watch needed the support of the King's Royal Rifle Corps, and the Highland Light Infantry that of the Duke of Cornwall's Light Infantry, from the reserve. Graham's brigade, supported by the guns, drove the Egyptians out of the trenches.",
        "Third, the other side of the canal. South of the canal, the Seaforth regiment attacked a redoubt, and the Indian brigade went around the Egyptian right and took a village, before entering Tel el-Kebir.",
        "Fourth, the cavalry. Drury Lowe went around the Egyptian left and came down through the rear to the canal bridge, to cut off the escape. Urabi's army fell apart. The battle had lasted about an hour.",
        "Fifth, Cairo. The cavalry reached Cairo on 14 September and received Urabi's surrender. The [[khedive|quediva]] Tawfiq returned to the capital on 25 September, escorted by British and Indian troops."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Egyptians lost about 2,000 dead and more than 60 guns (from 59 to 66, depending on the source). British losses, in the first tally, were 57 dead, 380 wounded and 22 missing. Later counts by the British War Office reach 83 dead and 607 wounded. In the campaign, the heat caused more casualties than enemy fire.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The [[khedive|quediva]] regained power, in practice under British tutelage. Gladstone's government first wanted to try and execute Urabi, but reduced the charges, and he admitted the revolt and was exiled. The British occupation, announced as temporary, lasted long: formal independence was declared in 1922, and the troops only left after the agreement of 1954. More than 70 years.",
        "With Egypt under British influence, London was dragged into Sudan. In 1885, in Khartoum, General Gordon would die in the war against the Mahdi.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Victoria Cross: Lieutenant William Mordaunt Marsh Edwards received the highest decoration of the British army for what he did in the battle." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  cartum: {
    war: "Mahdist Revolt in Sudan (1881–1899)",
    hook: "A British general sent to evacuate a city decided to stay, and the relief that England was waiting for arrived two days late.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Sudan was ruled by Egypt, which since 1882 had been under British influence. In 1881, a religious leader named Muhammad Ahmad declared himself the [[Mahdi|mahdi]] and raised the country against the Egyptians. In November 1883, near El Obeid, the Egyptian army was defeated.",
        "Gladstone's government did not want to send troops to Sudan. It decided to send a man: General Charles Gordon, with the mission of evacuating the garrisons of Sudan and the civilians loyal to Egypt.",
        { timeline: [
          ["November 1883", "The Egyptian army is defeated near El Obeid."],
          ["February 1884", "Gordon arrives in Khartoum."],
          ["13 March 1884", "The siege begins."],
          ["August 1884", "Under pressure from public opinion, Gladstone's government changes course and asks Parliament for a credit of 300,000 pounds for a possible relief force."],
          ["January 1885", "The relief column comes close, and the city falls."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Charles Gordon, British general" },
        "When he reached Khartoum, in February 1884, he found about 7,000 soldiers and 27,000 civilians. Instead of evacuating, as his instructions ordered, he decided to defend the city and committed himself to saving the other garrisons. He evacuated about 2,500 civilians and stayed with the soldiers and a few others. He wrote to the Mahdi, offering him the sultanate of Kordofan. The Mahdi replied by inviting him to convert to Islam and join him. Gordon refused.",
        "The British public admired his resistance. The government, which did not want him to stay, did not.",
        { h: "Muhammad Ahmad, the Mahdi" },
        "Religious and military leader. The army around Khartoum had about 30,000 men at the start of the siege and reached 50,000 at the end, according to the most cited estimates. On learning that the British relief was approaching, he resolved to attack before it arrived.",
        { h: "Garnet Wolseley" },
        "The same general of Tel el-Kebir was put in charge of the relief expedition. He divided the force in two: one column went up the Nile by boat, with about 400 Canadian boatmen, and the other, the Desert Column, under Herbert Stewart, crossed the Bayuda desert by camel, toward Metemma, where Gordon's steamers would be waiting."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The city" },
        "Khartoum lies at the junction of the Blue Nile and the White Nile. The Blue Nile protected the north side, and the White, the west. On the desert side, to the south, Gordon had trenches dug, improvised mines planted and wire strung. He set up a flotilla of nine small paddle steamers, with guns and metal plates, to guard the banks.",
        { h: "The siege" },
        "The tribes of the region went over to the Mahdi's side one by one, and the river and the telegraph were cut. Food ran out, and soldiers and civilians went hungry. The sorties and an attempt to reach Sennar failed. Requests for reinforcement were refused.",
        { note: "The date when Gladstone agreed to send the relief varies depending on what is called the decision. The credit of 300,000 pounds was requested from Parliament on 7 or 8 August 1884, but it was presented as a precaution, and some date the firm decision to send the expedition to September." },
        { h: "The relief" },
        "The British column took months to organize and only entered Sudan in January 1885. The Desert Column fought the Mahdists at Abu Klea and at Abu Kru, and Stewart was mortally wounded. Charles Wilson took command and reached the Nile near Metemma.",
        { h: "The night of the fall" },
        { note: "There is no single narrative of how the city fell. The accounts agree on the day and the hour, the end of the night of 25 to 26 January 1885, and disagree on the details." },
        "The Mahdists attacked the wall shortly before midnight. The Nile level was low, and they crossed the river on foot and went around the wall at the point where it reached the water. According to some accounts, by around 3:30 they were already inside the city. Another force, commanded by Al Nujumi, broke through the Massalamieh Gate, despite the mines and the wire. The defenders, weakened by hunger, resisted little.",
        "News reports of the time attributed the fall to the treachery of an Egyptian officer, Faragh Pasha, who was said to have opened a gate. These were accounts from refugees, and the modern sources we consulted do not confirm it.",
        { h: "The death of Gordon" },
        "There are three versions. In one, he came out of the palace in full uniform, refused to fight and was killed by a spear. In another, he was recognized as he tried to reach the Austrian consulate, which was neutral, and was shot in the street. In the third, his servant, in 1898, said that Gordon died fighting on the palace stairs, after being wounded in the shoulder. The head was taken to Omdurman, where Rudolf Slatin recognized it."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The garrison was massacred. About 4,000 civilian men were killed, and many women and children were enslaved. Two days later, two of Gordon's steamers, the Bordein and the Talahawiyeh, with about 240 men aboard, came within sight of the city. Wilson saw it was too late and went back to Metemma. He was criticized afterward for the delay.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Mahdi founded a state that lasted about 14 years. He died in June 1885, and Gladstone's government fell the same month. Gordon became a hero to a large part of the British public. Between 1896 and 1898, Kitchener reconquered Sudan, and the victory at Omdurman, in 1898, ended the Mahdist state.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Mahdi's numbers: estimates of the Mahdist army range from 30,000 to 50,000, and the force that attacked on the final night is estimated at 50,000." },
        { note: "The responsibility: the government did not want Gordon in Khartoum, the public wanted to save him, and the relief was only sent under pressure from public opinion and arrived two days late." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

